# Cards D: the food web, the life cycles that link pond and land, and the organisms of a drop of pond water.
from pond_lib import *

N('pond_food_web', 'The pond food web', 'process', 3, ['park_pond'],
  "Who eats whom among the organisms in this pond: sunlight feeds algae and plants, they feed grazers and the microbes that break down dead matter, and the grazers feed the hunters.",
  "A food web is a map of what eats what. In this pond it starts with sunlight. Algae, duckweed and hornwort turn light into food, and water fleas, tadpoles and snails feed on them or on bits of dead matter and bacteria. A dragonfly larva hunts the water fleas and tadpoles; a frog eats snails; and the pond skater, on top, eats small insects that land on the water. Everything that dies is broken down by bacteria, which return nutrients to the water for the plants and algae to use again. Only the organisms you can click in this pond are in the diagram; a real pond has many more.",
  "Energy flows one way through a food web: it enters as sunlight and, at each step from the grazers to the hunters, most is lost as heat, so there are always fewer hunters than grazers. Nutrients, by contrast, go round and round. Water fleas filter single-celled algae, detritus and bacteria; tadpoles eat algae and decomposed plants until their hind legs develop, then turn to animal food; a dragonfly larva eats aquatic insects, small tadpoles and other invertebrates and sometimes small fish; frogs eat invertebrates, slugs and snails in particular; tadpoles are in turn eaten by fish, diving beetles, dragonfly larvae and birds. A pond skater feeds on insects and small invertebrates on or just under the surface. In a great pond snail's case, diet in the wild is not covered here; in aquaria it eats leaves, vegetables and algae.",
  "The links drawn here come from the sources named on each organism's card; they are a small sample of real feeding relationships. Real food webs have many more links (omnivory, cannibalism, seasonal diet changes), and an organism's diet changes as it grows, as the tadpole's does. Missing from this diagram: the fish and diving beetles that eat tadpoles (not modelled), the birds, and the insects that land on the surface for the skater to eat.",
  [('Producers', 'algae, duckweed, hornwort (and the iris at the edge)'), ('Grazers and filter-feeders', 'water flea, tadpole, snail'), ('Hunters', 'dragonfly larva, pond skater, frog'), ('Recyclers', 'bacteria (and fungi) in the water and mud'), ('Energy', 'enters as sunlight; most is lost at each step')],
  'C1', [R_FLOW, R_COMM, R_ECO], [W_DAPH, W_FROG, W_AESH, W_GERRIS, W_LYM, ARC],
  parts=['pond_daphnia', 'pond_tadpole', 'pond_snail', 'pond_dragonfly_larva', 'pond_skater', 'pond_duckweed', 'pond_hornwort'], related=['bio_food_webs_energy_flow', 'bio_primary_production_decomposition', 'bio_ecosystem_stability', 'bio_biogeochemical_cycles_concept'], where='park_pond',
  fig=blocks([('sun', 'sunlight', 6, 12, None), ('prod', 'algae, duckweed, hornwort', 28, 12, None), ('det', 'dead leaves and matter', 20, 52, None), ('bac', 'bacteria (recyclers)', 20, 88, None),
              ('daph', 'water flea', 50, 28, None), ('snail', 'great pond snail', 50, 62, None), ('tad', 'tadpole', 72, 12, None), ('larva', 'dragonfly larva', 76, 40, None), ('frog', 'frog (adult)', 94, 62, None), ('skater', 'pond skater', 72, 86, None), ('out', 'insects that land on the water (not modelled)', 94, 92, None)],
             [('sun', 'prod', 'light'), ('prod', 'daph', None), ('prod', 'tad', None), ('prod', 'snail', None), ('det', 'bac', 'broken down by'), ('bac', 'daph', None), ('det', 'daph', None), ('daph', 'larva', None), ('tad', 'larva', None), ('snail', 'frog', None), ('out', 'skater', None)],
             'Who eats whom among the organisms in this pond. An arrow points from the food to the animal that eats it. Each link is from the source on that organism\'s card; it is a small sample, not the whole food web.'))

N('pond_life_cycles', 'Pond and land: the frog and the dragonfly', 'process', 1, ['park_pond'],
  "Frogs and dragonflies begin in the pond but spend their adult lives out of it: the pond is a nursery, and the land and air are where the adults live.",
  "Two animals in this pond link it to the land. A common frog lays its eggs in the pond in spring; they hatch into tadpoles that breathe with gills and grow legs over the summer, then leave the water as tiny froglets. Adult frogs live in damp places near ponds and return to breed, and in winter some hibernate in the mud at the bottom of ponds. A southern hawker dragonfly lays its eggs in plants at the water's edge; the larva spends two or three years under water, then climbs a plant out of the water at night and the adult emerges. Both animals therefore carry food and nutrients between the pond and the land.",
  "Common frogs hibernate from late October to January and may leave hibernation as early as February to migrate to garden ponds. They spawn from January in the south-west of England to as late as June, generally peaking in April; the eggs hatch in 2 to 4 weeks and the tadpoles begin growing hind legs about 16 weeks later and leave the water in early summer, sometimes as late as September. Adult frogs are up to 9 cm (males) and 13 cm (females) long. A southern hawker's larva is 38 to 48 mm long and moults between 6 and 15 times over two to three years; adults emerge in July and August and fly from June to October. The adult is a land-and-air predator, so the dragonfly moves energy from the pond's food web to the air.",
  "Where the animal sits in the web changes through its life. A tadpole is a plant-eater in the pond; the adult frog is a predator on land. A dragonfly larva is a top predator in the pond; the adult is an aerial one. Because the larva takes years, a pond that dries out or loses its plants loses a whole cohort. Common frogs 'hibernate in running water, muddy burrows or beneath decaying leaves and mud at the bottom of ponds or lakes', where the skin absorbs enough oxygen to sustain them (Wikipedia, Common frog).",
  [('Frog: from spawn to froglet', 'hatch in 2 to 4 weeks; hind legs after about 16 more weeks; leave the water early summer to September'), ('Frog: adult', 'up to 9 cm (male), 13 cm (female); damp places near ponds'), ('Dragonfly: larval life', 'two to three years; 6 to 15 moults'), ('Dragonfly: emergence', 'July and August; adults fly June to October')],
  'C1', [R_AMPH, R_ART, R_FLOW], [W_FROG, FROGLIFE, ARC, W_AESH, BDS, W_DRAGON], where='park_pond', parts=['pond_tadpole', 'pond_dragonfly_larva'], related=['bio_fishes_amphibians', 'bio_organoids_regeneration', 'bio_entomology_insects'],
  fig=blocks([('f1', 'frog spawn in the pond (January to June)', 12, 14, None), ('f2', 'tadpoles: gills, tail, eat algae', 38, 14, None), ('f3', 'hind legs, then front legs (about 16 weeks)', 64, 14, None), ('f4', 'froglet leaves the pond (early summer to September)', 90, 14, None), ('f5', 'adult frog on land; returns to breed', 90, 40, None),
              ('d1', 'eggs laid in plants at the water\'s edge', 12, 66, None), ('d2', 'larva in the pond: 2 to 3 years, 6 to 15 moults', 38, 66, None), ('d3', 'climbs a plant at night, adult emerges (July and August)', 64, 66, None), ('d4', 'adult flies (June to October); returns to breed', 90, 66, None)],
             [('f1', 'f2', None), ('f2', 'f3', None), ('f3', 'f4', None), ('f4', 'f5', None), ('d1', 'd2', None), ('d2', 'd3', None), ('d3', 'd4', None)],
             'Two life cycles that link pond and land. Timings are from Froglife, Wikipedia and the British Dragonfly Society; they vary with weather and place.'))

N('pond_drop', 'A drop of pond water', 'structure', 5e-4, ['pond_water'],
  "A single drop of pond water holds green algae, glass-shelled diatoms, single-celled hunters, tiny animals and bacteria: a small world, each member a different size.",
  "A drop of pond water looks like nothing, but under a microscope it teems with life. In the scene you see green algae (Chlamydomonas, and a ball-shaped colony called Volvox), diatoms with shells of glass, a Paramecium (a single cell that hunts), a rotifer (a tiny animal with a wheel of beating hairs) and many bacteria, with bits of dead plant. They are drawn against a 100 micrometre bar, one tenth of a millimetre, so you can see how much bigger some are than others: a bacterium is about 100 times shorter than the bar, and a rotifer about two and a half times longer. Click anything to find out what it is.",
  "These organisms make up the plankton of a pond: floating life too small to swim against currents (except by their own beating hairs). Green ones are producers, using light to make sugar; others eat bacteria, algae and dead matter; bacteria recycle what dies. The algae are what makes the water green when there are enough of them (see why ponds turn green). The scene is simplified: a real drop holds far fewer large organisms, and which species are present varies from pond to pond and season to season. A water flea, at about 2.5 mm, would be four times wider than this view.",
  "Sizes, with sources: Chlamydomonas reinhardtii about 10 µm (cells 10 to 22 µm long, 8 to 22 µm wide); Volvox carteri spheroid 100 to 500 µm with about 2,000 to 4,000 somatic cells; diatom cells 2 to 2,000 µm, most 20 to 200 µm; Paramecium 60 to 300 µm; rotifers mostly 100 to 500 µm; bacteria 0.5 to 5 µm. The scene draws each at one size within its range and uses far fewer individuals than a real drop has.",
  [('Scale bar', '100 µm = 0.1 mm'), ('Smallest', 'bacteria, 0.5 to 5 µm'), ('Largest in the scene', 'a Volvox colony and a rotifer, ≈ 250 µm'), ('Not in the scene', 'a water flea (≈ 2.5 mm) would span four such views')],
  'C1', [R_PROT, R_PROTG, R_GREEN, R_PROK], [W_CHLAMR, PMC_VOLVOX, W_DIAT, W_PARA, W_ROT, W_BACT],
  parts=['pond_volvox', 'pond_chlamydomonas', 'pond_diatom', 'pond_paramecium', 'pond_rotifer', 'pond_bacteria', 'pond_detritus'], related=['bio_protists', 'bio_algae_green_origin', 'bio_bacterial_structure', 'bio_biofilms_quorum', 'bio_microbial_ecology_elective'], where='park_pond',
  fig=bars([('bacterium', 2), ('Chlamydomonas', 10), ('round diatom', 40), ('pennate diatom', 70), ('Paramecium', 200), ('Volvox colony', 250), ('rotifer', 250), ('100 µm scale bar', 100)], 'size in micrometres', 'The organisms in the drop at the sizes drawn (each within its published range) beside the 100 µm bar.'))

N('pond_volvox', 'Volvox: a colony of cells', 'organism', 2.5e-4, ['pond_drop'],
  "Volvox is a hollow green ball, a few tenths of a millimetre across, made of thousands of small cells, each with two whip-like hairs, that rolls through the water.",
  "Volvox is a freshwater alga that lives as a colony: a hollow sphere whose surface is a single layer of thousands of small green cells. Each cell has two whip-like flagella pointing outward, and together they beat to make the ball spin and swim. A few larger cells inside are not for swimming: they divide to make new daughter colonies inside the ball, and the daughters are released when the parent breaks open. Volvox is found in ponds and ditches, even in shallow puddles.",
  "Volvox carteri colonies (spheroids) are 100 to 500 µm across, commonly 150 to 300 µm; the shell has about 2,000 to 4,000 biflagellate cells and about 16 large cells that make the next generation (12 to 16 in another source). The genus overall has colonies of up to tens of thousands of cells. The small cells are Chlamydomonas-type cells, and they carry chlorophyll; the colony is a classic example of how a many-celled body may have evolved from single cells like Chlamydomonas.",
  "Colonies are drawn 250 µm across with 70 cells shown; the real number is about two thousand or more. Each small cell has two flagella of equal length, facing outward; this is not drawn at this scale. During development each embryo turns itself inside out (inversion) so that its flagella point outward.",
  [('Colony diameter (V. carteri)', '100 to 500 µm; drawn 250 µm'), ('Cells in the surface layer', '≈ 2,000 to 4,000 (V. carteri)'), ('Reproductive cells', 'about 12 to 16'), ('Each surface cell', 'two flagella, pointing outward')],
  'C1', [R_GREEN, R_PROTG], [PMC_VOLVOX, W_VOLVOX, X('Volvox carteri (Wikipedia)', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Volvox_carteri')], parts=[], related=['bio_algae_green_origin', 'bio_protists'], where='park_pond',
  fig=bars([('smallest spheroids', 100), ('typical (centre of one sample)', 225), ('drawn', 250), ('largest', 500)], 'colony diameter, micrometres', 'Size of Volvox carteri colonies (PMC10923841): 100 to 500 µm, with most between 150 and 300 µm.'))

N('pond_chlamydomonas', 'Chlamydomonas: a single green cell', 'organism', 1e-5, ['pond_drop'],
  "A single-celled green alga about 10 micrometres across that swims with two whip-like flagella and steers towards light.",
  "Chlamydomonas is one of the smallest green things in a pond: a single cell, about a hundredth of a millimetre across. It swims with two whiplike flagella at the front. Inside is a cup-shaped green chloroplast, where light is turned to sugar, and a red-orange eyespot that helps the cell find the light. It lives in still water and damp soil. The green pigment that catches the light is chlorophyll a; press the button to see the molecule.",
  "The cell is about 10 µm, with a single large cup-shaped chloroplast holding a pyrenoid where starch forms; the two flagella are each 1.5 to 2 times the length of the cell body. The eyespot sits near the cell's equator and, by changing the balance of the beating of the two flagella, steers the cell towards or away from light (phototaxis). The genus lives in stagnant water and damp soil, and generally prefers ammonium-rich environments.",
  "C. reinhardtii: 10 to 22 µm long, 8 to 22 µm wide. The cell is drawn as a 10 × 12 µm ellipsoid.",
  [('Size', 'about 10 µm (10 to 22 µm long)'), ('Flagella', 'two, each 1.5 to 2 cell-lengths long'), ('Chloroplast', 'one, cup-shaped, with a pyrenoid'), ('Pigment shown', 'chlorophyll a')],
  'C1', [R_GREEN, R_PHOTO], [W_CHLAMR, W_CHLAM], parts=[], related=['bio_algae_green_origin', 'bio_chloroplasts_plastids', 'photosynthesis_light_process'], where='park_pond')

N('pond_diatom', 'Diatoms: algae in glass houses', 'organism', 7e-5, ['pond_drop'],
  "A diatom is a single-celled alga that lives in a box of glass: a hard, patterned shell made of silica.",
  "Diatoms are algae enclosed in a shell of glass-like material, silica (the same substance as quartz sand, but in a hydrated form). The shell has two halves that fit together like a lid on a box and is covered with fine holes and patterns. Diatoms come in many shapes: boat-shaped ones and round, drum-shaped ones both appear in the scene. They make food from light like other algae. Press the button to see silicic acid, the dissolved form of the material their shell is made from.",
  "Each diatom cell is enclosed in a hard, porous wall of silica (hydrated silicon dioxide) called a frustule. Diatoms with radial symmetry are called centric, and those with bilateral symmetry are pennate. When a diatom divides each daughter gets one half of the frustule and makes a new, smaller half, so the average size of a population slowly falls until sexual reproduction restores it. Diatoms produce about 20 to 50 % of the oxygen produced on the planet each year, though estimates of their share of ocean production differ: 45 % in a 2003 study and about 20 % in a 2016 one.",
  "Cells are 2 to 2,000 µm, mostly about 20 to 200 µm; about 12,000 species have been described, with higher estimates for the number that exist. The scene draws a boat-shaped cell 70 µm long and a round one 40 µm across.",
  [('Size', '2 to 2,000 µm; most 20 to 200 µm'), ('Shell', 'silica (hydrated silicon dioxide), the frustule'), ('Shapes', 'centric (round) and pennate (boat-shaped)'), ('Share of Earth\'s oxygen made', 'about 20 to 50 % a year (estimates differ)')],
  'C2', [R_PROTG, R_PHOTO], [W_DIAT], openq=['What share of the planet\'s oxygen and of ocean photosynthesis diatoms are responsible for is estimated differently by different studies, from about 20 % to about 50 %.'],
  parts=[], related=['bio_protists', 'bio_algae_green_origin'], where='park_pond')

N('pond_paramecium', 'Paramecium: a hunter that is one cell', 'organism', 2e-4, ['pond_drop'],
  "Paramecium is a slipper-shaped single cell, 0.06 to 0.3 mm long, covered in beating hairs that swim it along and sweep bacteria into its mouth groove.",
  "A Paramecium is one cell, yet it swims, hunts and digests. It is covered in rows of short beating hairs (cilia) that drive it through the water and sweep in the microbes it eats: bacteria, algae and yeasts. They are swept into a groove and then into the cell, where they are digested. A Paramecium is a protist, which is not a plant, animal or fungus.",
  "Cilia sweep prey and some water into an oral groove; food then passes through a small opening into the cell. Because pond water is less salty than the cell's inside, water flows in all the time, and contractile vacuoles pump it out again. The macronucleus is polyploid and controls the cell's everyday functions, apart from reproduction.",
  "The genus ranges from 0.06 to 0.3 mm (60 to 300 µm); the scene shows a 200 µm cell with a 40 µm macronucleus. The numbers of cilia and vacuoles vary with the species and are not drawn.",
  [('Length (genus)', '60 to 300 µm; drawn 200 µm'), ('Eats', 'bacteria, algae and yeasts'), ('Moves with', 'rows of cilia'), ('Pumps out water with', 'contractile vacuoles')],
  'C1', [R_PROT, R_PROTG], [W_PARA], parts=[], related=['bio_protists'], where='park_pond')

N('pond_rotifer', 'Rotifer: a tiny animal with a wheel of hairs', 'organism', 2.5e-4, ['pond_drop'],
  "A rotifer is a tiny many-celled animal, often a quarter of a millimetre long, with a ring of beating hairs round its mouth that sweeps in food.",
  "Rotifers are animals, but most are no longer than half a millimetre. At the front is a crown of beating hairs, the corona, that sweeps food in and, in free-swimming kinds, pulls the animal through the water. Behind the mouth is a muscular throat with tiny hard jaws. A rotifer eats dead matter, bacteria, algae and single-celled animals up to about 10 micrometres across. Rotifers are an important part of the floating animal life of fresh water.",
  "Most rotifers are about 0.1 to 0.5 mm, with a range from about 50 µm to over 2 mm. The corona is ciliated in all but one genus; the muscular chewing pharynx (mastax) holds calcified jaws (trophi), the only parts that fossilise. An adult has a fixed number of cells, usually of the order of 1,000 (eutely). Rotifers reproduce sexually or by parthenogenesis, which in many kinds dominates the life cycle and allows rapid population growth.",
  "The scene draws a 250 µm rotifer with a 16 µm corona; the shape is simplified.",
  [('Length', 'mostly 0.1 to 0.5 mm; drawn 0.25 mm'), ('Cells', 'about 1,000, fixed in adults'), ('Eats', 'detritus, bacteria, algae, protozoans up to 10 µm'), ('Reproduces', 'sexually or by parthenogenesis')],
  'C1', [R_ART, R_MOL], [W_ROT], parts=[], related=['bio_lophotrochozoans'], where='park_pond')

N('pond_bacteria', 'Bacteria in the water', 'organism', 2e-6, ['pond_drop'],
  "The smallest living things in the drop: single cells 0.5 to 5 micrometres long, in huge numbers, feeding on dissolved and dead matter.",
  "Bacteria are the smallest living things in the scene, drawn as tiny rods. They have no nucleus. Most are rod-shaped or round. They feed on dissolved matter and on the dead plants and animals in the water, and they are food for Paramecium and rotifers. Without them the dead would never be broken down and their nutrients would not be returned for plants and algae to use again.",
  "Bacterial cells are typically 0.5 to 5 µm in length. Bacteria take part in many stages of nutrient cycles, including fixing nitrogen from the air, and are responsible for the breakdown of dead matter. In soil, a few grams hold around a thousand million of them; the scene draws only about 130.",
  "Cells in the scene are drawn 1.4 × 3.2 µm, which is within the range.",
  [('Cell length', 'typically 0.5 to 5 µm'), ('Shapes', 'mostly spheres or rods; also curved and spiral'), ('Role', 'recycle dead matter and nutrients')],
  'C1', [R_PROK, R_CYC], [W_BACT], parts=[], related=['bio_bacterial_structure', 'bio_bacterial_diversity', 'nitrogen_cycle_process'], where='park_pond')

N('pond_detritus', 'Detritus: bits of dead matter', 'structure', 1e-5, ['pond_drop'],
  "Flecks of dead plant and animal matter drifting in the water: food for rotifers, water fleas and bacteria.",
  "Detritus is the name for bits of dead matter. In pond water it is broken leaf, plant debris and the remains of dead organisms, drifting or settling. Many small animals eat it, and bacteria live on it and break it down. It is an important link in the food web: energy first captured by plants is passed on from dead matter to the animals and microbes that feed on it.",
  "Water fleas eat detritus as well as algae, protists and bacteria; rotifers eat detritus and dead bacteria. Detritus also settles to become the rotting matter in the mud (see Fallen leaves). The scene draws 18 flecks, 20 µm long; a real drop holds many more of varied sizes.",
  "The flecks in the scene are drawn at about 20 × 6 × 13 µm. They are illustrative.",
  [('Eaten by', 'water fleas, rotifers (and broken down by bacteria)')], 'C1', [R_ECO, R_FLOW], [W_DAPH, W_ROT], parts=['pond_leaf_litter'], related=['bio_primary_production_decomposition'], where='park_pond')
